    The declining double declines instead of throwing
    
    The CardReader contract says who is wrong. A card that can't be charged
    comes back false, and charge() never throws. buyCoffee() already handles
    false. The double broke the contract, so the fix goes in the double.
    
    The compiler didn't catch it. It checks that the double has a charge()
    that returns a boolean, not what charge() does. A double only stands in
    for the real reader if it keeps the contract every CardReader guarantees.
    That is Liskov substitution, applied to a test double. A double that
    breaks it tests buyCoffee() against a card reader that can't exist.
    
    The tempting fix was in buyCoffee(): catch the exception and call it a
    decline. The test would pass, but buyCoffee() would then be written for
    that impossible card reader, and any real bug inside charge() would
    reach the customer as "card declined".

